out 50 to  100 additional backs mobile vaccination  sites so that can be added here and then  when they're done with them they can be  removed from the model  so you can see users can  come in and check or uncheck and then  they can save the list of vaccine sites  once that is done they can go over to  the proposed distribution tab this  allows you the first user input here we  have is number of days because of the  complexity of the model we needed to  ascertain how many days does the  ministry have to allocate these number  of vaccines so they can decide here that  we have these current batches and based  on the expiration date we want to  allocate all of these vaccines in the  next 20 days or 30 days they are the  default we've used is 30 days Days based  on the ministry's feedback and they can  save this information and then run the  model so once  all of this information is saved we  click this button to run the model which  will  produce your proposed distribution Maps  so this takes a few seconds  but this is essentially a linear  optimization model with  um  in with constraints around  expiration date  time to vaccinate  eligible population we will walk through  the list of constraints  in a bit so this is the proposed this is  the distribution map that is generated  by the app you can look at the map and  then  if you  hover over any of the vaccine sites it  will show you the name  the regional Depot and the district  Depot that site is connected to when the  number of vaccines of each type  allocated to That vaccine site so here  we can see that this site has been  allocated only Ceno farm so 720 two  doses of Sino farm and none of the other  ones then we can see here this site has  been allocated 960 doses of Pfizer  so so on and so forth you can click on  any of the site and look at how many  noses have been allocated  to that particular site  then there's the map below where um of  course the above map was a little  difficult to read so you can filter  um this map on whichever District you  want to look at and here we've  selected abobo East and you can zoom in  and look at the number of vaccines  allocated to each of the vaccination  site connected to this District  Distribution Center  so yeah this is basically the model once  this  is done you can users can go over to  results tab where they can view all of  these results in a tabular format they  can see which how many doses of each  type of vaccine has been assigned to  each vaccination site they can download  these results share it with their theme  and make decisions based on this  then we just have a tab for model  documentation  but this is  um  the  shiny app for vaccine allocation tool we  still haven't launched it next month we  are going to have a training session  with our Ministry of Health and their  covid-19  covid-19  team in country and once this is done we  will be  transitioning it off to the team so that  they can update and use this  um tool for making decisions allocation  decisions going forward  so like I said this is essentially a  linear optimization  um  problem where our main focus was our  decision variable was to figure out how  many vaccines should be allocated to  each site  and um  the optimization objective was to  minimize wastage and while maximizing  the number of people vaccinated in the  eligible population and we ran into we  started with a smaller list of  constraints but over time we ran into  um you know as you work with the data  you figure out uh  some additional constraints so it  included that  we had to rank prioritize batches based  on their time to expiration so not only  did we have four different types of  vaccines for each type of vaccine you  could have multiple batches so we had to  make sure that the model starts to  allocate the batches that expire first  before it allocates batches that have  more time to expiration then  um we uh yeah we had to there were a lot  of constraints around expiration date  making sure that it's  um uh delivered and administered before  its expiration date that there are  enough people to administer those  vaccines at the site  so yeah this is um the  um  our vaccine allocation  tool there was a lot of hesitancy from  the ministry initially about using R and  are shiny as a lot of you might know  that normally in public health care  especially in Africa countries tend to  use dhis2 platform so they were  extremely hesitant to you know work with  a new platform a separate one for their  covid-19 vaccination means but once they  saw what we did to bring them on board  was that before we even got any data  from the country we built a mock app we  travel to the country we had a training  uh requirements Gathering Workshop we  showed them the app they were able to  use it and see how useful it can be for  their decision making and that was  essentially all we needed to get them on  board and since then they have been  um extremely active and instrumental in  ensuring that we receive all the data  that we need we have regular meetings  with the Ministry of Health folks and  their covid-19 task force to ensure that  they are abreast with all the changes  that are happening that we are making to  the model  and um we are still figuring out some of  the challenges with uh transitioning  this tool over to the country team like  I said you know they  this is a new tool for them they don't  have our programmers on their team so we  will we're continuing to work with the  ministry  to figure out those challenges  but yeah and  that's where my talk ends I is I would  like to advertise  um this a little bit since I got a  chance that I am hiring a senior  associate in data science ours is a  small but growing theme and we work on  very interesting problems we don't have  huge data sets but we do have uh we do  build very interesting uh models so do  get in touch with me if you have any if  you're interested  great it's like anybody  [Music]


